313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (Sir Fazle Hasan Abed exemplified servant leadership- a model in which the leader's primary motivation is to serve others rather than exercise power or accumulate personal benefits. Unlike transactional leaders who use incentives and rewards to motivate employees, or charismatic leaders who inspire through personal magnetism, Abed inspired BRAC staff through shared mission and collective purpose. He believed that meaningful work serving the poor was intrinsically motivating. According to BRAC's institutional memory, Abed fostered a culture where staff felt genuine "pride and ownership" in their work, viewing themselves as part of a historic movement for social change. He called BRAC staff "Abed Bhai" \(brother\) - a term of familial respect- emphasizing that leadership was not hierarchical command but collaborative service. His humility was legendary, despite founding one of the world's largest organizations, he maintained close connections with field staff and remained open to challenges to his ideas. This servant leadership approach proved remarkably effective in sustaining institutional commitment and institutional memory even as BRAC grew to massive scale.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 164 0 R
/T (Visionary Pragmatism)
/E (Visionary Pragmatism)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
315 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (Abed's leadership style combined visionary idealism with ruthless pragmatism. His famous maxim- "Small is beautiful, scale is necessary"- captures this tension. He believed in the beauty and potential of small communities and local solutions, yet he insisted that truly addressing poverty required scaling interventions to reach millions. This philosophy prevented BRAC from becoming either a small, boutique development organization or a massive bureaucracy divorced from ground realities. Abed used evidence-based analysis to ground his vision in reality. He invested heavily in research and evaluation not as an afterthought but as a core organizational function. When BRAC decided to enter microfinance, he didn't import models wholesale from other countries; instead, his team rigorously studied what worked for poor Bangladeshi women. This generated insights that became influential globally. His pragmatism manifested in a willingness to use business mechanisms to achieve social goals; a controversial position for traditional NGOs at the time. Abed didn't view making profit as contradictory to social mission; rather, he saw it as the means to achieve financial independence and sustainability.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
316 0 obj
<<
