330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E (Abed possessed a comprehensive vision of poverty alleviation that went far beyond conventional charity. Rather than treating poverty as a single problem with a single solution, he understood it as a complex phenomenon requiring multi-dimensional interventions. His vision encompassed education, health, microfinance, agriculture, skills development, and gender empowerment- all integrated into a coherent system. Crucially, this vision was grounded in research. BRAC's Research and Evaluation Department didn't merely measure outcomes; it generated strategic insights about the underlying causes of poverty in different contexts. This research orientation allowed BRAC to continually refine and adapt its approach, pioneering innovations like the Graduation approach for ultra-poor households- an evidence-based model that has now become influential globally and influenced government policies in multiple countries.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
331 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E (Innovation and Risk-Taking)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
332 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E (Abed was a strategic risk-taker willing to venture into uncharted territory. When BRAC decided to enter microfinance in the 1980s, this was considered risky for an NGO- the assumption was that poor women couldn't reliably repay loans. BRAC's microfinance program not only proved this assumption wrong but became one of the world's largest microfinance operations, eventually growing into BRAC Bank, which now serves hundreds of thousands of clients. Similarly, Abed's decision to establish social enterprises including Aarong \(a fair-trade craft enterprise\), BRAC Bank, and BRAC University was innovative for its time. These weren't peripheral activities but core to his strategy for achieving financial independence from donor funding. By generating revenue from social enterprises and program-related investments, BRAC reduced dependency on donor whims and gained autonomy in strategic decision-making. This approach proved prescient; many NGOs became vulnerable in later years when donor funding dried up, but BRAC's diversified revenue streams provided resilience. Abed's willingness to fail and learn from failure was also critical. Not all BRAC initiatives succeeded; some were discontinued after rigorous evaluation showed insufficient impact. This commitment to evidence over sentiment distinguished BRAC from organizations that became attached to pet projects regardless of effectiveness.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
