350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 168 0 R
/E (Abed's approach to social enterprise was both pragmatic and visionary. He recognized that traditional NGO models, dependent on donor funding, face chronic sustainability challenges. Donor priorities shift, funding becomes unpredictable, and organizations lose strategic autonomy. Abed's solution was to embed for-profit social enterprises within BRAC's ecosystem. These enterprises are commercially viable, generating profits while advancing social mission. Unlike traditional corporate social responsibility initiatives, BRAC's enterprises aren't philanthropy; they're legitimate businesses designed to scale impact and achieve financial independence. The classic example is Aarong, BRAC's fair-trade craft enterprise. Aarong employs thousands of artisans \(predominantly women\) in rural Bangladesh, providing fair prices, market access, and holistic support services. Through Aarong's retail outlets and e-commerce, customers in developed countries purchase authentic Bangladeshi crafts while supporting rural livelihoods. Aarong is profitable, reinvesting surpluses into community programs. This model has inspired similar initiatives globally and demonstrates how business can be a vehicle for social change.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
