353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 168 0 R
/E (BRAC's entry into microfinance in the mid-1980s was transformative. At the time, mainstream development thinking questioned whether poor women could reliably repay loans. BRAC's rigorous research and careful program design proved this assumption wrong. BRAC's microfinance program focused specifically on women in poverty, providing not just loans but complementary services: business training, group formation, literacy classes, and health education. This holistic approach achieved repayment rates exceeding 98%, comparable to commercial banks. The program scaled dramatically, reaching millions of borrowers across Bangladesh and internationally. Recognizing the potential for financial self-sufficiency, BRAC eventually established BRAC Bank in 2001, a regulated financial institution offering banking services at commercial rates. BRAC Bank became profitable, paying dividends while reaching underserved populations. This transition from donor-dependent program to self-sustaining financial institution exemplifies Abed's vision of achieving development impact through business mechanisms.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 168 0 R
/E (Beyond Aarong and BRAC Bank, Abed expanded social enterprises into diverse sectors. BRAC University provides quality higher education to talented poor students, combining affordability with academic excellence. BRAC Food and Agriculture Enterprise works to improve agricultural productivity among small farmers through improved seed varieties and farming techniques. BRAC Health ventures provide essential healthcare services in underserved areas at affordable prices. These )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
