/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (While BRAC's social enterprises achieved significant scale, they didn't entirely eliminate donor dependency for the NGO's core development programs. Education programs in ultra-poor areas, for instance, operate at a loss; they're subsidized by commercial enterprises and donor funds. This raises legitimate questions: Has BRAC genuinely achieved financial independence? Could the current scale of impact be maintained if donor funding evaporated? Abed was transparent about these constraints, understanding that certain types of development work \(serving the ultra-poor in remote areas\) may never be fully self-financing. However, critics argue that the emphasis on social enterprise occasionally diverted resources from the poorest populations, who cannot generate immediate commercial returns.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
