/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (Sir Fazle Hasan Abed succeeded where many development leaders did not because of a distinctive combination of qualities. First, he held a deep conviction in the potential of poor communities and an unwavering commitment to serving them; this was not a career move but a defining life choice. Second, he balanced idealism with pragmatism, pairing a strong vision for social transformation with careful reliance on evidence and practical action. Third, he understood that lasting impact depends on building strong institutions, not simply delivering services, and he invested in organizational capacity, staff development, and systems even when these efforts were not immediately visible to donors. Fourth, he recognized that financial sustainability is essential for independence and introduced innovative social enterprise approaches to achieve it. Fifth, he upheld ethical integrity in a context often marked by corruption, establishing an organization widely respected for its honesty and transparency. Sixth, he cultivated a mission driven culture that attracted and retained talented individuals despite limited financial incentives, because the work itself carried purpose and meaning. Finally, he appreciated the importance of evidence and continuous learning, embedding research and evaluation into the core of the organization rather than treating them as secondary concerns. Together, this blend of vision, practicality, institutional strength, financial innovation, ethical leadership, strong culture, and commitment to evidence made his approach exceptionally effective.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
